        <div id="find-drafts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How to Find Drafts Around a Door Before You Buy Anything
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best weatherstripping for doors is the one that fits the gap you actually have, so the first job is finding where the air gets in. Most drafty doors leak in two or three places, not all the way round, and each place needs a different product. Ten minutes with the tests below tells you whether to buy jamb seal, a sweep, a new threshold or all three.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Light Test</h3>

          <p>
            On a bright day, or at night with a light on outside, close the door, turn off the lights indoors and look at the edges. Any line of light along the top, the sides or the bottom is a gap that air can pass through. Mark each spot with painter's tape so you can measure it later. Light under the door means a sweep or door bottom; light along the latch side means the jamb seal is worn or missing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Paper Test</h3>

          <p>
            Close the door on a sheet of paper in several places along the frame. If the paper slides out with no resistance, the door is not pressing on its weatherstripping there. This finds gaps that do not show light, such as a compressed seal along the hinge side or a corner where the frame has moved.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Smoke or Hand Test</h3>

          <p>
            On a windy, cold day, hold the back of your damp hand or a lit incense stick close to the edges of the closed door. Moving air will cool your hand or bend the smoke. Do this carefully and never near curtains. It also finds leaks around the trim and the lock, which no weatherstripping fixes; those need caulk, which our <a href="/best-caulk-for-windows/">guide to caulk</a> covers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Measure the Gap</h3>

          <p>
            Once you have found the gaps, measure them. A stack of coins, a set of feeler gauges or a folded card works. Write down the smallest and largest gap along each side. That range decides the profile: thin tape for small gaps, D-profile or V-strip for medium ones, thick foam or an adjustable kit for large or uneven ones.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. V-Strip vs Foam vs Silicone vs Rubber: Which Door Weatherstripping Type?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Door weatherstripping is sold in a confusing number of shapes and materials. In practice, four types cover almost every home door, and the choice comes down to the size and shape of your gap and how long you want the seal to last.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Foam Tape</h3>

          <p>
            Foam is the cheapest and easiest seal: peel it, stick it to the door stop and close the door on it. It fills irregular gaps well because it squashes wherever it is pressed. The trade-off is life span. Open-cell and soft foams take a permanent set, harden in sun and cold, and usually need replacing every season or two. Choose foam for renters, for big uneven gaps and for doors you rarely open.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">EPDM Rubber D- and P-Profiles</h3>

          <p>
            Hollow rubber tape, sold by profile shape, is the best all-round choice for exterior doors. The letter describes the cross-section. A D-profile is a hollow half-round that suits medium gaps of roughly the size of its thickness; a P-profile is smaller with a flat tab, for tighter doors. Because the profile is hollow it recovers each time the door opens, and rubber lasts much longer than foam.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Silicone</h3>

          <p>
            Silicone seals look like rubber profiles but stay flexible across a wider range of temperatures, which makes them the better choice for doors that face direct sun or hard frost. They also resist taking a set. They cost more per foot, and many listings give only the length, so check the profile dimensions before you buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">V-Strip (Tension Seal)</h3>

          <p>
            V-strip is a folded strip of plastic or metal that fits inside the jamb, between the door edge and the frame, and springs open to fill the gap. It is ideal for older doors whose gap changes along the frame, and it is almost invisible once fitted. It needs a smooth, flat surface and careful fitting, and it makes the door close a little firmer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Screw-On Kits</h3>

          <p>
            Aluminum or wood strips carrying a vinyl or foam seal are screwed to the frame so the seal presses against the door face. They outlast adhesive tape on busy doors and can be adjusted as the door settles, but they are visible and take tools to fit.
          </p>

