        <div id="sweep-vs-threshold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Door Sweep vs Threshold vs Door Bottom: Sealing Under the Door
          </h2>

          <p>
            The gap under an exterior door is often the worst leak in the doorway, and it is where people most often buy the wrong part. Three products seal the bottom of a door, and they work together rather than replacing each other.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Threshold</h3>

          <p>
            The threshold, or saddle, is the strip on the floor that the door closes over. Many exterior thresholds are adjustable, with screws under a cap that raise the top, and some carry their own vinyl insert. If yours is cracked, rotted or sunk, no sweep will seal against it reliably; repair or replace it first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Door Bottom</h3>

          <p>
            A door bottom is a U-shaped or kerf-style seal attached to the bottom edge of the door itself. Many modern steel and fiberglass entry doors use a kerf door bottom, with fins pushed into slots cut into the door. When those fins tear or flatten, replacing the door bottom is the right fix, and you need to match the slot spacing and door thickness.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Door Sweep</h3>

          <p>
            A door sweep is a strip of aluminum, wood or vinyl with a flexible vinyl, rubber or brush seal that screws to the face of the door so the seal brushes the threshold. It is the easiest of the three to add to an older door with no door bottom, and the adjustable slots let you set the height exactly. The Frost King A59/36H in our picks is this type.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which One Do You Need?</h3>

          <p>
            If light shows under the door and the threshold is sound, add a sweep. If the door already has a torn door bottom, replace that. If the threshold is damaged or there is a gap of more than about 3/4 inch, fix the threshold first. For the large door into a garage, see our guide to <a href="/best-garage-door-bottom-seal/">garage door bottom seals</a> instead; it is a different part.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-weatherstrip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Weatherstrip a Door: Step by Step
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fitting adhesive weatherstripping to a door takes about half an hour and a few cheap tools: a tape measure, sharp scissors or a utility knife, rubbing alcohol and a rag. A screw-on kit adds a drill or screwdriver and a hacksaw.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Remove the Old Seal</h3>

          <p>
            Pull off old foam or rubber and scrape away the leftover adhesive. A plastic scraper and an adhesive remover help, and so does a hair dryer on stubborn glue. If the old seal sits in a slot in the frame, pull it straight out; that frame takes a kerf-style replacement, not tape.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Clean and Warm the Surface</h3>

          <p>
            Wipe the face of the door stop with rubbing alcohol and let it dry. Adhesive does not bond to dust, grease or a cold, damp frame. Duck, for example, says to apply its foam above 50 degrees F. In winter, heat the room for a while first or work on a mild day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Fit the Head, Then the Sides</h3>

          <p>
            Start with the top piece, then run each side down from the top. Peel a few inches of liner at a time and press the seal onto the stop where the closed door touches it, not on the surface the door slides past. Do not stretch the seal as you go; stretched tape shrinks back and opens gaps at the corners.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Make Tight Corners</h3>

          <p>
            Butt the side pieces firmly against the head piece so there is no gap at the top corners, which is where many drafts start. Trim the bottom of each side so it stops just above the threshold.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Close and Check</h3>

          <p>
            Close the door and check that it latches without forcing. If it will not latch, the seal is too thick; use a thinner profile or adjust the strike plate slightly. Repeat the paper test from earlier. A sound seal grips the paper all the way round.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="door-sweep-install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How to Install a Door Sweep
          </h2>

          <p>
            A screw-on sweep is one of the quickest door upgrades there is, but the height setting decides whether it works.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Measure and Cut</h3>

          <p>
            Measure the width of the door between the stops and cut the sweep to that length with a hacksaw, cutting the vinyl with scissors. Most sweeps are sold at 36 inches, so a narrower door needs trimming.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Set the Height</h3>

          <p>
            Close the door. Hold the sweep against the inside face with the seal lightly touching the threshold, and mark the screw slots with a pencil. The seal should brush the threshold, not be crushed against it, or the door will drag and the seal will wear quickly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fix and Adjust</h3>

          <p>
            Drill small pilot holes at the marks, especially on wood doors, and drive the screws at the middle of each slot. Open and close the door a few times, then slide the sweep up or down and tighten. The slots let you lower it later as the seal wears.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Outswinging Doors</h3>

          <p>
            On a door that opens outward, the sweep goes on the outside face, where it will see weather. Check the maker's instructions for that use, and choose an aluminum sweep rather than wood.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-door" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The Best Weatherstripping for Each Kind of Door
          </h2>

          <p>
            The same product rarely suits every door in a house. Here is how we would match them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Front and Back Entry Doors</h3>

          <p>
            A hollow rubber or silicone D-profile on the stops, plus a sweep or a new door bottom underneath, seals most entry doors. On a busy door where tape keeps peeling, use a screw-on kit like the M-D aluminum kit instead.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Older Wood Doors</h3>

          <p>
            Old wood doors swell and shrink with the seasons and rarely have an even gap. V-strip handles that variation best, because it keeps springing outward as the gap opens. Pair it with a sweep, since old doors seldom have a door bottom.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Steel and Fiberglass Doors</h3>

          <p>
            Most prehung steel and fiberglass doors use a kerf-style seal in a slot in the frame, and a kerf door bottom. Replace those with matching kerf parts rather than sticking tape over them. Bring a piece of the old seal to compare.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Interior Doors</h3>

          <p>
            Doors to a cold basement, a garage or an unheated room benefit from a slide-on door bottom like M-D's Cinch, and sometimes foam tape on the stops. Interior doors are usually 1-3/8 inch thick, so check the size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Patio and Sliding Doors</h3>

          <p>
            Sliding doors use pile or fin seals in tracks and are a different job. Our <a href="/best-window-insulation-kit/">window insulation kit guide</a> covers kits sized for patio doors for winter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="renters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Weatherstripping a Door When You Rent
          </h2>

          <p>
            Renters can still seal a drafty door; the trick is choosing products that leave no damage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choose Clean-Removal Seals</h3>

          <p>
            Duck's expanding foam is listed as removing cleanly, which Duck says makes it suitable for renters. Many rubber and silicone tapes leave adhesive behind, and that can lift paint when you pull them off. Test a short piece in a hidden spot first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use Slide-On and Freestanding Stoppers</h3>

          <p>
            A slide-on door bottom, like the M-D Cinch, attaches without screws or glue and comes off in seconds. A fabric draft snake also works for a door you rarely use, but it moves every time the door opens.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ask the Landlord</h3>

          <p>
            A worn threshold, a torn door bottom or a door that no longer latches tightly are maintenance problems for the landlord. Report them in writing, and ask before fitting anything that screws into the door or frame.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lifespan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Long Does Door Weatherstripping Last?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every door seal wears out, and knowing the signs saves you from sitting in a draft for another winter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Typical Life by Material</h3>

          <p>
            Makers rarely state a life span, and use matters more than age. As a rule of thumb, soft foam is the shortest-lived and often needs replacing each season or two; rubber and silicone profiles last several years; screw-on kits and aluminum sweeps outlast the vinyl inserts they carry, which can usually be replaced.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Signs It Is Time to Replace It</h3>

          <p>
            Replace a seal that is flattened, cracked, crumbling, peeling off the stop or no longer grips a sheet of paper when the door is closed. Light under the door, or a sweep that no longer touches the threshold, means it is time to adjust or replace it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Make It Last</h3>

          <p>
            Clean seals with mild soapy water rather than solvents, keep paint off them when you repaint the frame, and check the door hinges. A sagging door crushes the seal on one side and opens a gap on the other; tightening the hinge screws, or fitting a longer screw into the stud, often fixes both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="whole-house" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Beyond the Door: Sealing the Rest of the House
          </h2>

          <p>
            A sealed door makes a room feel warmer quickly, but doors are only one of the places a house loses heat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Windows</h3>

          <p>
            Drafty windows leak in the same ways as doors. Rubber or foam tape works on the sashes, and heat-shrink film is a cheap winter fix; see our <a href="/best-window-insulation-kit/">window insulation kit guide</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Garage Door</h3>

          <p>
            If you have an attached garage, insulating and sealing the big door keeps the space next to your living area warmer. Our <a href="/best-garage-door-insulation-kit/">garage door insulation kit guide</a> and <a href="/best-garage-door-bottom-seal/">garage door bottom seal guide</a> cover both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pipes and Cold Rooms</h3>

          <p>
            Pipes in unheated spaces near outside walls are at risk once you seal and the house holds heat differently; insulate them with the products in our <a href="/best-pipe-insulation/">pipe insulation guide</a>. For one cold room, an <a href="/best-oil-filled-radiator-heater/">oil-filled radiator</a> gives steady, quiet heat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heating Controls</h3>

          <p>
            Sealing a door changes how quickly rooms warm and cool. A <a href="/best-smart-thermostat/">smart thermostat</a> lets you set a schedule that matches, so the heating is not running harder than it needs to.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Weatherstripping and Ventilation: What Not to Seal
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealing drafts is good for comfort, but a few gaps are there for a reason.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fuel-Burning Appliances</h3>

          <p>
            Gas furnaces, water heaters, fireplaces and wood stoves need air to burn and vent safely. If you seal a room that holds one, follow the appliance maker's instructions and local codes, and make sure you have working carbon monoxide alarms. If a fuel-burning appliance is in a closet or small room with a door, do not seal that door without checking its air supply requirements.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fire Doors</h3>

          <p>
            The door between an attached garage and the house is often required to be a fire-rated, self-closing door. Do not add anything that stops it closing and latching on its own, and check local code before changing its seals.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Moisture</h3>

          <p>
            A tightly sealed house holds more moisture. If windows start running with condensation after you seal up, use bathroom and kitchen fans and keep an eye on humidity.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Before Measuring the Gap</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A seal too thick stops the door latching; one too thin does nothing. Measure the smallest and largest gap along each side, then choose the profile that matches.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sticking Seal to the Wrong Surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Jamb seal goes on the face of the door stop, where the closed door presses on it, not on the surface the door edge slides past.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stretching the Tape as You Apply It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stretched tape shrinks back and opens gaps at the corners. Lay it on relaxed and butt the side pieces tightly against the head piece.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding a Sweep to a Broken Threshold</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A sweep only seals against a sound threshold. Fix a cracked, sunk or rotted threshold first, or the draft stays.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing a Room With a Fuel-Burning Appliance</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Furnaces, water heaters and stoves need combustion air. Follow the maker's instructions and local codes, and keep carbon monoxide alarms working.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best weatherstripping for doors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most exterior doors, a hollow rubber D-profile tape such as the Frost King V25WA on the door stops is the best weatherstripping: it springs back each time the door closes and outlasts foam. Choose M-D's silicone gasket for doors facing harsh weather, M-D V-Flex for uneven gaps, and add a sweep like the Frost King A59/36H for the gap under the door.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best way to weatherstrip a door?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Find the gaps first with the light and paper tests, then measure them. Remove the old seal, clean the door stop with rubbing alcohol, and apply the new seal to the face of the stop, starting at the head and working down each side without stretching it. Finish with a sweep or new door bottom, and check that the door still latches.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is V-strip better than foam?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For doors that are opened every day, V-strip usually lasts longer, because polypropylene or metal keeps its spring while foam flattens and hardens. V-strip also handles gaps that change along the frame. Foam is cheaper and easier to fit, and better on rough surfaces and for big, irregular gaps. Many people use foam as a quick fix and V-strip or rubber as the lasting one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a door sweep and a threshold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A threshold is the strip on the floor that the door closes over. A door sweep is attached to the face of the door and brushes against the threshold to seal the gap. A door bottom is a seal attached to the bottom edge of the door. If the threshold is damaged, fix it first; a sweep only seals against a sound threshold.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is silicone or rubber weatherstripping better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Silicone generally stays flexible across a wider temperature range and resists taking a set, so it suits doors exposed to hard frost or strong sun. EPDM rubber is cheaper, widely available in D- and P-profiles, and lasts well on most doors. On a sheltered door, rubber is fine; on a south-facing or exposed door, silicone is worth the extra cost.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop a draft under my front door?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If light shows under the door, add a screw-on sweep set so the seal just brushes the threshold. If the door has a torn kerf-style door bottom, replace it with a matching part. Adjustable thresholds can be raised with the screws under the cap. A gap larger than about 3/4 inch usually means the threshold needs repair or replacement.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you weatherstrip a door in winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but adhesive seals need a clean, dry, reasonably warm surface. Duck, for example, says to apply its foam above 50 degrees F. Heat the room first, clean the stop with rubbing alcohol, and press the tape firmly. Screw-on kits and sweeps can be fitted in any weather, which makes them a good choice for a cold doorframe.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

